## Mantiq prototypes a path from swarm overviews to individual agent actions

Source: Alex Strick van Linschoten — https://alexstrick.com/posts/2026-10-31-swarm-observability.html
Published: 2026-10-31
Channel: hn

Mantiq is Alex Strick van Linschoten’s prototype system for recording and exploring groups of AI agents working together. It combines model and tool calls, operating-system process starts, file reads and writes, messages between agents, and container output. The author’s starting problem is that an agent’s account of its work may be wrong, while failures can appear across several kinds of evidence.

The viewer first gives investigators an overview of the swarm. They can filter for an observed problem and see the agents involved, then move from swarm to team to individual agent. Zooming further reaches a message or file edit. Another view stacks teams horizontally and time vertically, making simultaneous failures appear as a layer that investigators can select for detail.

The article demonstrates a small team building a Lisp interpreter and prototypes views over a synthetic 10,000-agent swarm. These are interface experiments, rather than evidence of production operation at that scale. The author says explanatory views still need more work for complex interactions; the accompanying analysis agent also generates some explanations, so those interpretations remain distinct from the recorded events.

Why it matters: For teams investigating multi-agent work: evaluate whether a viewer connects high-level patterns to recorded messages, files and processes, and distinguish generated explanations from those records.

