## OpenInference records model and token counts for OpenAI image calls

Source: Arize OpenInference · GitHub — https://github.com/Arize-ai/openinference/releases/tag/python-openinference-instrumentation-openai-v0.1.69
Published: 2026-10-31
Channel: github

OpenInference’s Python OpenAI instrumentation records model interactions as trace spans, records of individual operations that tools such as Arize and Phoenix can inspect. Its image-call update addresses missing accounting inputs: image generation, editing and variation spans previously lacked the model and token-count attributes those tools use for cost calculations.

First, the instrumentor reads input, output and total token counts from the response’s usage information. It records them for synchronous and asynchronous calls, including when the returned image list is empty. For streamed generation and editing, the final completion event supplies usage. Next, it takes the model name from the request when the caller explicitly supplied one; image responses do not provide it.

The change does not record the separate text and image input-token counts. The pull request explains that these can have different prices, and accurate pricing needs a corresponding semantic-convention addition. Responses with no usage information remain unchanged. The author reports comparing stored Phoenix spans against real generation responses for synchronous, asynchronous and streaming calls; that verification supports captured fields, rather than complete billing accuracy across every image operation.

Why it matters: For teams tracking image-generation spend: verify the new fields reach stored spans, and check pricing assumptions before treating combined input-token counts as an accurate bill.

