## OpenInference preserves document structure and unknown values in Anthropic traces

Source: Arize OpenInference · GitHub — https://github.com/Arize-ai/openinference/releases/tag/python-openinference-instrumentation-anthropic-v3.3.0
Published: 2026-10-31
Channel: github

OpenInference’s Python Anthropic instrumentation records model requests and responses for AI trace inspection. Its update changes how documents and interrupted streams appear in those records. Documents previously stored as generic JSON text gain dedicated fields, while unfinished tool calls stop acquiring misleading empty arguments.

For documents, the instrumentor first identifies the source type. Inline encoded documents become data URLs with a media type; linked PDFs retain their URL; plain text becomes verbatim document content; uploaded files use a document identifier. A supplied title becomes the document name. Input and output messages share this mapping, while nested content and unknown source types retain generic recording.

For streams, the instrumentor tracks which content blocks started and finished. If a tool block remains unfinished, it omits its arguments instead of serializing the SDK’s empty placeholder. Completed blocks retain their arguments, including genuinely empty ones. It also withholds completion and total token counts until output usage is known, rather than treating the initial placeholder as final. The linked implementation and regression tests cover these distinctions; this is a correction to the recorded evidence, not a claim that interrupted work completed successfully.

Why it matters: For Anthropic trace consumers: handle absent arguments and token totals as unknown, and review document-content retention settings when adopting the structured fields.

